# INFORMATION HIERARCHY

Every important decision screen should approximately answer in this order:

WHAT YOU ASKED

↓

WHAT TRUECOST UNDERSTOOD

↓

WHAT WAS COMPARED

↓

WHAT WON

↓

TRUECOST FINAL KNOWN COST

↓

WHY IT WON

↓

WHAT THE TRADEOFF IS

↓

WHAT WE KNOW / DON'T KNOW

↓

FRESHNESS / COVERAGE / EVIDENCE

Progressive disclosure is critical.

A user should not need to read the entire screen to understand the decision.

Power users must still be able to inspect deeper information.

---

# DATA TRUTH — ABSOLUTE RULE

NEVER sacrifice truth for visual beauty.

If:

price = null

Do NOT display:

₹0

Display something truthful such as:

Unavailable
Not verified
Unknown

If provider data is unavailable:

do not fabricate a quote.

If entity matching is rejected:

do not visually claim “Same Product.”

If only some providers were compared:

do not say “Best everywhere.”

Use language such as:

Best known option
Best among 3 compared providers

where appropriate.

If one fee is unknown:

do not silently assume zero.

Unknown must remain unknown.

A beautiful false statement is a P0 failure.

---

# TRUST UI

Trust must be visible, not hidden in legal text.

Where appropriate show:

* providers compared
* provider coverage
* last updated / freshness
* exact / probable / uncertain matching state
* known vs unknown costs
* unavailable provider
* partial evidence
* why the recommendation won
* tradeoff
* relevant assumptions

Do this elegantly.

Do not turn every card into an engineering diagnostics screen.

Use progressive disclosure.

---

# ACCESSIBILITY FROM DAY ONE

Accessibility is NOT a final cleanup phase.

Every component must support it from the moment it is created.

Validate:

* appropriate minimum interactive target size
* font scaling
* screen reader labels
* logical reading order
* contrast
* no color-only meaning
* Reduce Motion
* reduced transparency where appropriate
* keyboard/focus behavior
* safe areas
* gesture areas
* long content
* small screens
* dynamic text

States such as:

BEST
UNKNOWN
STALE
UNAVAILABLE
ERROR
EXACT

must not rely only on color.

---

# PERFORMANCE FROM DAY ONE

Performance is part of the visual design.

Target a smooth 60fps baseline and excellent high-refresh behavior where hardware supports it.

Prefer:

* transforms
* opacity
* UI-thread animation
* virtualization
* efficient lists
* image caching
* lazy loading
* memoization where justified
* measured blur usage
* optimized assets

Avoid:

* dozens of simultaneously animated elements
* expensive continuous blur
* unnecessary JS-thread synchronization
* huge image assets
* layout recalculation every frame
* effects that reduce interaction responsiveness

Do not certify performance from development/debug behavior alone.

Use production/release-like builds where practical.

